## Step 9 — Edit document page

`app/documents/[id]/edit/page.tsx` loads the document and pre-fills the form:
```tsx
import { createClient } from '@/lib/supabase/server'
import { updateDocument } from '@/app/actions'
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'
import Link from 'next/link'

export default async function EditDocument({ params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }) {
  const { id } = await params
  const supabase = await createClient()
  const { data: doc } = await supabase
    .from('documents')
    .select('id, title, client, due_date, status, assignee_id')
    .eq('id', Number(id))
    .single()

  if (!doc) notFound()

  return (
    <main className="max-w-md mx-auto p-8">
      <Link href="/" className="text-blue-600">← Back</Link>
      <h1 className="text-xl font-bold my-4">Edit document</h1>
      <form action={updateDocument} className="space-y-3">
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value={doc.id} />
        <input name="title" defaultValue={doc.title} required className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <input name="client" defaultValue={doc.client ?? ''} className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <input name="due_date" type="date" defaultValue={doc.due_date} required className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <select name="status" defaultValue={doc.status} className="border p-2 w-full">
          <option value="pending">pending</option>
          <option value="in_progress">in_progress</option>
          <option value="completed">completed</option>
        </select>
        <input name="assignee_id" type="number" defaultValue={doc.assignee_id ?? ''} className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <button className="bg-blue-600 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded">Update</button>
      </form>
    </main>
  )
}
```
In Next 15 `params` is a Promise, hence `await params`. `defaultValue` pre-fills each field; the `id` travels in a hidden input so `updateDocument` knows which row to update.

## Step 10 — Authentication (real login)

**a) Enable Email auth.** Supabase → **Authentication → Providers → Email** (enabled). Under **Authentication → Users → Add user** create a test user (email + password).

**b) Middleware to refresh the session.** `lib/supabase/middleware.ts`:
```ts
import { createServerClient } from '@supabase/ssr'
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export async function updateSession(request: NextRequest) {
  let response = NextResponse.next({ request })
  const supabase = createServerClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!,
    {
      cookies: {
        getAll() { return request.cookies.getAll() },
        setAll(cookiesToSet) {
          cookiesToSet.forEach(({ name, value }) => request.cookies.set(name, value))
          response = NextResponse.next({ request })
          cookiesToSet.forEach(({ name, value, options }) =>
            response.cookies.set(name, value, options))
        },
      },
    }
  )
  const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser()
  if (!user && !request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith('/login')) {
    const url = request.nextUrl.clone()
    url.pathname = '/login'
    return NextResponse.redirect(url)
  }
  return response
}
```
`proxy.ts` (project root):
```ts
import { type NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { updateSession } from '@/lib/supabase/middleware'

export async function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  return await updateSession(request)
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ['/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico|.*\\.(?:svg|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|webp)$).*)'],
}
```
The proxy refreshes the token on every request (Server Components can't write cookies) and redirects to `/login` if there's no session.

> **Next.js 16:** the `middleware.ts` file was deprecated and renamed to `proxy.ts` (and the `middleware` function → `proxy`). It's just a rename; same behavior. Important: a `middleware.ts` left un-renamed is silently ignored, so route protection would stop working. Migrate with `npx @next/codemod@latest middleware-to-proxy .`. The helper `lib/supabase/middleware.ts` can keep its name (it's not a convention file).

**c) Login page.** `app/login/page.tsx`:
```tsx
'use client'
import { createClient } from '@/lib/supabase/client'
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'
import { useState } from 'react'

export default function Login() {
  const supabase = createClient()
  const router = useRouter()
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('')
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('')
  const [error, setError] = useState('')

  async function handleLogin(e: React.FormEvent) {
    e.preventDefault()
    const { error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithPassword({ email, password })
    if (error) { setError(error.message); return }
    router.push('/')
    router.refresh()
  }

  return (
    <main className="max-w-sm mx-auto p-8">
      <h1 className="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Log in</h1>
      <form onSubmit={handleLogin} className="space-y-3">
        {error && <p className="text-red-500 text-sm">{error}</p>}
        <input type="email" value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)}
          placeholder="Email" required className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <input type="password" value={password} onChange={e => setPassword(e.target.value)}
          placeholder="Password" required className="border p-2 w-full" />
        <button className="bg-blue-600 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded w-full">Log in</button>
      </form>
    </main>
  )
}
```

**d) Tighten RLS.** Now that there is login, replace the dev policies. For a shared internal tool (all employees see all documents):
```sql
drop policy "dev_all_docs" on documents;
drop policy "dev_all_resp" on assignees;
create policy "auth_all_docs" on documents for all
  using (auth.uid() is not null) with check (auth.uid() is not null);
create policy "auth_all_resp" on assignees for all
  using (auth.uid() is not null) with check (auth.uid() is not null);
```
> If you want each user to see only their own rows: add a column `owner_id uuid default auth.uid()` and use `using (auth.uid() = owner_id)`.

## Step 11 — Navigation map (how it all links)

- `/` (`app/page.tsx`): the list. Links to `/new` and `/documents/[id]/edit`; Delete and Log out are `<form>`s → Server Actions. With no session, the middleware redirects to `/login`.
- `/new` (`app/new/page.tsx`): form → `createDocument` → back to `/`.
- `/documents/[id]/edit`: pre-filled form → `updateDocument` → back to `/`.
- `/login` (`app/login/page.tsx`): `signInWithPassword` → `/`. Log out (`logout`) brings you back here.
- All mutations live in `app/actions.ts`.

```
app/
├── actions.ts          # Server Actions (create/update/delete/logout)
├── page.tsx            # list + navigation (protected)
├── login/page.tsx      # login
├── new/page.tsx        # create
└── documents/[id]/edit/page.tsx   # edit
lib/supabase/{client,server,middleware}.ts
middleware.ts
```

## Step 12 — Deploy to Vercel
1. Push the project to GitHub (`git init`, commit, push).
2. vercel.com → **Add New → Project** → import the repo.
3. **Environment Variables**: paste `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`.
4. **Deploy** → in ~1 min you get `https://...vercel.app` accessible from Japan and Vietnam.

Every `git push` redeploys automatically.

## Step 13 — Test → production flow

Vercel provides this flow by default, on top of git branches:

- **Preview (test):** every `push` to a non-`main` branch, or every Pull Request, generates a deployment with its **own unique URL**. That is your test environment.
- **Production:** only a `push`/merge to `main` updates the production URL. Nothing reaches production without going through it.

Typical flow:
```bash
git checkout -b feature/new-screen
# ...changes...
git push origin feature/new-screen   # → Vercel creates a Preview automatically
# review on the preview URL; if OK:
git checkout main
git merge feature/new-screen
git push origin main                  # → updates PRODUCTION
```
With a Pull Request (recommended in a team): open PR → Vercel comments the preview link → review → merge to `main` → production.

**Per-environment variables (key).** In Vercel → **Settings → Environment Variables** each variable is assigned to **Production**, **Preview**, and/or **Development** separately. Recommended: **two Supabase projects** (one test, one production); Preview variables point to the test DB and Production variables to the real one. This lets you test without touching real data.

Notes:
- The Hobby (free) plan already includes unlimited previews.
- You can protect previews with login so they aren't public.
- For automated tests before deploy, it integrates with GitHub Actions.

## Step 14 — Migrations and environments (schema as code)

The idea that avoids copy-pasting: the schema lives as **migration files** in your repo; each environment is rebuilt from them. You never copy production data; preview data is seed data.

Initialize Supabase and capture the current schema as a migration:
```bash
npx supabase init
npx supabase login
npx supabase link --project-ref <PROD-ref>
npx supabase db pull          # creates supabase/migrations/<ts>_remote_schema.sql
```
Commit that file. For each future change:
```bash
npx supabase migration new add_something
# write the change's SQL in the generated file
```

### Option B — two synced projects (free)

1. Create a second Supabase project (e.g. `staging`) in Tokyo; note its URL and anon key.
2. Apply the migrations to each environment by re-linking:
```bash
# staging
npx supabase link --project-ref <STAGING-ref>
npx supabase db push
# production (after validating on staging)
npx supabase link --project-ref <PROD-ref>
npx supabase db push
```
3. Test data on staging only: sample INSERTs in `supabase/seed.sql` (or in its SQL Editor). Production stays untouched.
4. In Vercel → **Settings → Environment Variables**, assign per environment:
   - **Production** → production URL + anon key.
   - **Preview** (and Development) → staging URL + anon key.

Result: preview deployments use the staging DB; production uses the real one. You sync the **schema** (migrations), never the data.

> Security: never commit the DB password or connection strings.
> Automated alternative: **Supabase Branching** does this automatically per PR (isolated preview + Vercel variables), but requires a paid plan (~$0.013/branch/hour).

## Step 15 — Responsive UI, search, filters and pagination

**a) Shared style tokens.** `lib/ui.tsx` centralizes the Tailwind classes used everywhere (inputs, buttons, labels, links) plus a `StatusBadge` component, so `/`, `/new`, `/documents/[id]/edit` and `/login` look consistent instead of each page hand-rolling its own classes:
```tsx
export const inputClass =
  'w-full rounded-md border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 focus:border-blue-500 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500/30 dark:border-gray-700 dark:bg-gray-950 dark:text-gray-100'

const STATUS_STYLES: Record<string, string> = {
  pending: 'bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 dark:bg-amber-900/40 dark:text-amber-300',
  in_progress: 'bg-blue-100 text-blue-800 dark:bg-blue-900/40 dark:text-blue-300',
  completed: 'bg-green-100 text-green-800 dark:bg-green-900/40 dark:text-green-300',
}

export function StatusBadge({ status }: { status: string }) {
  const style = STATUS_STYLES[status] ?? 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-800 dark:text-gray-300'
  return <span className={`inline-flex rounded-full px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium ${style}`}>{status.replace('_', ' ')}</span>
}
```

**b) Responsive table.** Instead of a separate mobile "card" layout (more code, two render paths to keep in sync), secondary columns collapse by breakpoint:
```tsx
<th className="hidden px-4 py-3 sm:table-cell">Client</th>
<th className="hidden px-4 py-3 md:table-cell">Assignee</th>
```
Title, due date, status and actions stay visible at every width; the table also sits in an `overflow-x-auto` wrapper as a safety net for very narrow viewports.

**c) Search and filter — no client component needed.** A plain GET `<form>` submits straight to `/?q=...&status=...`; the browser does the navigation, no `onChange`/`useState`/debounce required:
```tsx
<form action="/" className="mb-4 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-end">
  <input name="q" defaultValue={q} placeholder="Title or client…" className={inputClass} />
  <select name="status" defaultValue={status} className={inputClass}>
    <option value="">All statuses</option>
    {STATUSES.map((s) => <option key={s} value={s}>{s.replace('_', ' ')}</option>)}
  </select>
  <button className={buttonPrimaryClass}>Filter</button>
</form>
```
`Home` reads `q`/`status`/`page` from `searchParams` (a `Promise` in the App Router) and builds the query:
```ts
const { q = '', status = '', page: pageParam } = await searchParams
const page = Math.max(1, Number(pageParam) || 1)

let query = supabase
  .from('documents')
  .select('id, title, client, due_date, status, assignees(name)', { count: 'exact' })
  .order('due_date', { ascending: true })

// Strip characters with special meaning in PostgREST's or() syntax
// (comma separates conditions, parens group them) so a search term
// can't break the filter.
const safeQ = q.replace(/[,()]/g, '').trim()
if (safeQ) query = query.or(`title.ilike.%${safeQ}%,client.ilike.%${safeQ}%`)
if (status) query = query.eq('status', status)
```

**d) Pagination.** `{ count: 'exact' }` on the `select` plus `.range(from, to)` returns both the page of rows and the total count in a single request:
```ts
const PAGE_SIZE = 10
const from = (page - 1) * PAGE_SIZE
const { data: documents, count, error } = await query.range(from, from + PAGE_SIZE - 1)
const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil((count ?? 0) / PAGE_SIZE))
```
`Previous`/`Next` are plain `<Link>`s built by a small helper that carries `q` and `status` forward and only adds `page` to the URL when it isn't 1 (so page 1 stays a clean `/`):
```ts
function pageHref({ q, status, page }: { q: string; status: string; page: number }) {
  const sp = new URLSearchParams()
  if (q) sp.set('q', q)
  if (status) sp.set('status', status)
  if (page > 1) sp.set('page', String(page))
  const qs = sp.toString()
  return qs ? `/?${qs}` : '/'
}
```

> Why no Client Component (first pass): search, filter and paging were all expressible as a GET form and links — the same server-first approach as the rest of the app. No `useState`, no debounce, and it kept working with JavaScript disabled. `q`/`status`/`page` living in the URL also made the current view bookmarkable and shareable. Step 16 revisits this: live, as-you-type search needs a Client Component after all.

## Step 16 — Delete confirmation, cursor feedback, and live search across all fields

**a) Delete needs a confirmation.** A destructive action behind a single click is a footgun. The fix is a tiny Client Component wrapping just the submit button — the surrounding `<form action={deleteDocument}>` (the Server Action) doesn't change:
```tsx
// app/DeleteButton.tsx
'use client'

export function DeleteButton({ className }: { className?: string }) {
  return (
    <button
      type="submit"
      className={className}
      onClick={(e) => {
        if (!confirm('Delete this document? This cannot be undone.')) {
          e.preventDefault()
        }
      }}
    >
      Delete
    </button>
  )
}
```
`confirm()` blocks until the user answers; canceling calls `e.preventDefault()` on the click, which stops the `<button type="submit">` from submitting its form. No modal library needed for a one-off confirmation like this.

**b) Buttons didn't look clickable.** Only `<a>` gets a pointer cursor by default in most browsers — a plain `<button>` shows the regular arrow cursor unless you say otherwise. One global rule in `app/globals.css` fixes every button in the app at once, instead of adding `cursor-pointer` to every className string:
```css
button:not(:disabled) {
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

**c) Searching every field — and the PostgREST limit we hit.** The original search only checked `title`/`client`. Widening it to `status` was trivial (another `ilike` in the same `or()`), but the `assignees.name` and `due_date` columns exposed a real PostgREST constraint, confirmed by testing directly against the REST API before writing any code:
```bash
# column::type casts are rejected *inside* an or() filter:
curl ".../documents?or=(title.ilike.%25x%25,due_date::text.ilike.%252026%25)"
# → 400 PGRST100 "unexpected \":\" ... failed to parse logic tree"
```
So `due_date` can't be substring-searched this way (dates rarely benefit from that anyway). `assignees.name` lives on a joined table, and `or()` can't filter an embedded resource's column directly — so it's resolved with a small lookup first, then matching documents by the resulting ids:
```ts
const orParts = [
  `title.ilike.%${safeQ}%`,
  `client.ilike.%${safeQ}%`,
  `status.ilike.%${safeQ}%`,
]
const { data: matchingAssignees } = await supabase
  .from('assignees').select('id').ilike('name', `%${safeQ}%`)
if (matchingAssignees?.length) {
  orParts.push(`assignee_id.in.(${matchingAssignees.map((a) => a.id).join(',')})`)
}
query = query.or(orParts.join(','))
```

**d) Live search, debounced.** Typing should update the results without clicking a "Filter" button — that needs a Client Component, since it requires reacting to every keystroke. `app/Filters.tsx` replaces the old GET `<form>`:
```tsx
'use client'
import { usePathname, useRouter, useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation'
import { useRef, useState, useTransition } from 'react'

const DEBOUNCE_MS = 350

export function Filters({ defaultQ, defaultStatus }: { defaultQ: string; defaultStatus: string }) {
  const router = useRouter()
  const pathname = usePathname()
  const searchParams = useSearchParams()
  const [q, setQ] = useState(defaultQ)
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition()
  const debounceRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null)

  function navigate(nextQ: string, nextStatus: string) {
    const params = new URLSearchParams(searchParams.toString())
    if (nextQ) params.set('q', nextQ); else params.delete('q')
    if (nextStatus) params.set('status', nextStatus); else params.delete('status')
    params.delete('page')
    startTransition(() => router.replace(`${pathname}?${params.toString()}`))
  }

  function handleQChange(value: string) {
    setQ(value)
    if (debounceRef.current) clearTimeout(debounceRef.current)
    debounceRef.current = setTimeout(() => navigate(value, /* current status */ ''), DEBOUNCE_MS)
  }
  // ...status select calls navigate(q, value) immediately on change, no debounce needed
}
```
`useTransition` marks the URL update as low-priority so typing stays responsive, and drives a small "Searching…" indicator via `isPending`. Debouncing means the Server Component (and the Supabase query) only re-runs ~350ms after the user stops typing, not on every keystroke.

One easy-to-miss detail: the component's local `q`/`status` state needs to follow the props when the URL changes from *outside* the component (browser back/forward, a pasted link). Syncing them in a `useEffect` triggers React's `set-state-in-effect` lint rule (cascading renders) — the fix is adjusting state directly during render, which React explicitly documents as safe for this exact case:
```tsx
const [prevDefaultQ, setPrevDefaultQ] = useState(defaultQ)
if (defaultQ !== prevDefaultQ) {
  setPrevDefaultQ(defaultQ)
  setQ(defaultQ)
}
```

> Net effect: three of four pieces (delete confirmation, cursor, and the wider search predicate) needed no new client-side state at all. Only "search as you type" genuinely requires a Client Component — everything else stays server-first.

## Step 17 — Loading states for the slow spots

`/` and `/documents/[id]/edit` both await a Supabase query before they can render anything (the list + count + assignee lookup on one, a `.single()` row fetch on the other) — so navigating in or out of them has a real gap where nothing happens on screen yet. That network round trip can't be removed, but the *perceived* wait can: the App Router's `loading.tsx` file convention wraps a route segment in a `<Suspense>` boundary automatically, so Next.js shows that file immediately on navigation and swaps in the real page the moment the data resolves. No client state, no manual spinner wiring — just naming the file correctly:

```tsx
// app/loading.tsx — shown while Home's Supabase query is in flight
export default function Loading() {
  return (
    <main className="mx-auto max-w-5xl animate-pulse p-4 sm:p-8">
      <div className="h-8 w-56 rounded bg-gray-200 dark:bg-gray-800" />
      {/* ...skeleton bars shaped like the toolbar and table rows... */}
    </main>
  )
}
```
```tsx
// app/documents/[id]/edit/loading.tsx — shown while the row fetch is in flight
export default function Loading() {
  return (
    <main className="mx-auto max-w-md animate-pulse p-4 sm:p-8">
      {Array.from({ length: 5 }).map((_, i) => (
        <div key={i} className="mb-4 h-10 rounded-md bg-gray-200 dark:bg-gray-800" />
      ))}
    </main>
  )
}
```

Each skeleton is sized to match its real page (same max-width, same number of rows/fields) so the content doesn't jump once it swaps in. `/new` and `/login` don't get one — they're already static (prerendered at build time), so there's no data-fetching gap for a `loading.tsx` to cover.

> Note: this only smooths *route-to-route* navigation (clicking a `<Link>` between `/`, `/new`, `/documents/[id]/edit`). The live search in Step 16 already handles its own in-place loading via `Filters`' `isPending` + "Searching…" indicator, wrapped in `useTransition` specifically so it *doesn't* re-trigger this route-level fallback on every keystroke.

## Step 18 — Sortable columns and double-click-to-edit

**a) Sortable headers — no JS needed.** Like pagination, "sort by this column" is just a link to a different URL; clicking a header navigates to `/?sort=title&dir=asc`. `sort`/`dir` join `q`/`status`/`page` as URL state, so the current view stays bookmarkable:
```ts
const SORT_COLUMNS = {
  title: { column: 'title' },
  client: { column: 'client' },
  due_date: { column: 'due_date' },
  status: { column: 'status' },
  assignee: { column: 'name', referencedTable: 'assignees' },
} as const

type SortKey = keyof typeof SORT_COLUMNS

const sortConfig = SORT_COLUMNS[sort]
query = query.order(sortConfig.column, {
  ascending: dir === 'asc',
  referencedTable: 'referencedTable' in sortConfig ? sortConfig.referencedTable : undefined,
})
```
Sorting by `assignee` orders by a column on the *joined* `assignees` table, not `documents` itself — supabase-js's `.order()` takes a `referencedTable` option for exactly this (verified against the real API first: `?order=assignees(name).asc` → `200`). `foreignTable` is the same option under its old, deprecated name.

Each header is a small module-level component — not one declared inside `Home`, which would recreate it (and lose its identity) on every render:
```tsx
function SortHeader({ sortKey, label, sort, dir, q, status }: { sortKey: SortKey; label: string; sort: SortKey; dir: 'asc' | 'desc'; q: string; status: string }) {
  const nextDir = sort === sortKey && dir === 'asc' ? 'desc' : 'asc'
  const isActive = sort === sortKey
  return (
    <Link href={buildHref({ q, status, sort: sortKey, dir: nextDir, page: 1 })} className="inline-flex items-center gap-1">
      {label}
      {isActive && <span aria-hidden>{dir === 'asc' ? '▲' : '▼'}</span>}
    </Link>
  )
}
```
Clicking an inactive column sorts it ascending; clicking the already-active one flips direction. Changing the sort resets to page 1, same as changing a filter.

**b) Double-click a row to edit it — the one part that needs a Client Component.** `dblclick` is a browser event; there's no link/form equivalent. A thin wrapper around `<tr>` is enough — everything else about the row (the cells, the Edit link, the Delete form) stays exactly as it was:
```tsx
// app/DocumentRow.tsx
'use client'
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'

export function DocumentRow({ href, className, children }: { href: string; className?: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const router = useRouter()
  return (
    <tr
      className={className}
      onDoubleClick={(e) => {
        if ((e.target as HTMLElement).closest('a, button')) return
        router.push(href)
      }}
    >
      {children}
    </tr>
  )
}
```
The `closest('a, button')` guard matters: without it, double-clicking the row's own **Edit** link or **Delete** button would *also* fire the row's navigation — harmless for Edit (same destination), but worth avoiding for Delete, where a stray navigation racing the `confirm()` dialog is just needless complexity.

> Same pattern as Step 16: reach for a Client Component only for the one interaction that's inherently a browser event (`dblclick`, keystrokes) — sorting and pagination stay server-rendered links.

---

**Summary:** Supabase = ready-made backend (DB + API + auth); Vercel = global front end + test→prod flow on git. You only write pages and queries. That's why it's the fastest and cheapest option compared to running a VM.